2. Lesson Plan
Learning Objective
Students will understand how JavaScript objects store key-value data and how JSON is used to store and transfer structured data.
By the end of the lesson, students will be able to:
Create JavaScript objects.
Access object properties.
Explain the difference between JavaScript objects and JSON.
Identify basic JSON syntax rules.
Convert JavaScript objects into JSON strings.
Parse JSON strings into JavaScript objects.
Work with nested objects and arrays.
Success Criteria
You can:
Create an object using key-value pairs.
Access a property using dot notation.
Explain why an undefined property does not necessarily cause an error.
Identify the rules that make JSON different from JavaScript objects.
Use JSON.stringify() to create a JSON string.
Use JSON.parse() to turn JSON into a JavaScript object.
Create a nested object containing an array.
Print your object as JSON.
Tech Talk — 5 Minutes
A JavaScript object stores related information using key-value pairs.
For example:
const character = {
    name: "Steve",
    level: 10,
    class: "Warrior"
};

The keys are name, level, and class.
You can access values using dot notation:
console.log(character.name);
console.log(character.level);

JSON stands for JavaScript Object Notation.
JSON looks similar to a JavaScript object, but JSON is a text format for storing and transferring data.
JSON has stricter rules:
Keys must use double quotes.
Strings must use double quotes.
Functions are not allowed.
Comments are not allowed.
Trailing commas are not allowed.
JavaScript objects and JSON work together using:
JSON.stringify()

to convert an object into JSON, and:
JSON.parse()

to convert JSON back into a JavaScript object.

