4. Code Examples
A. Simple: JavaScript Object
A JavaScript object stores related information using key-value pairs.
%%js

const student = {
    name: "Steve",
    level: 10,
    class: "Warrior"
};

console.log(student);

Try It
Change the student's:
Name
Level
Class
Then run the code again.

B. Code Runner 1: Access Object Properties
%%js

// CODE_RUNNER: Personalize your character profile screen.

const character = {
    name: "Steve",
    level: 10,
    class: "Warrior"
};

console.log("Name:", character.name);
console.log("Level:", character.level);
console.log("Class:", character.class);

Try It
Change the character's name to your own.
Change the level.
Change the class.
Log the entire object.
Then try:
console.log(character.element);

This prints undefined because element was never defined.
JavaScript objects are flexible. Accessing a property that does not exist returns undefined.

C. JSON Rules
JSON stands for JavaScript Object Notation.
JSON is not JavaScript code. It is a text format used to store and transfer data.
Valid JSON looks like this:
{
    "name": "Steve",
    "level": 10,
    "class": "Warrior",
    "online": true
}

JSON Rules
JSON:
Requires double quotes around keys.
Requires double quotes around strings.
Does not allow functions.
Does not allow comments.
Does not allow trailing commas.
Uses values such as strings, numbers, booleans, arrays, objects, and null.

D. Code Runner 2: Parse JSON
%%js

// CODE_RUNNER: Personalize your character sheet and parse it from JSON.

const jsonData = `
{
    "name": "Steve",
    "level": 10,
    "class": "Warrior",
    "online": true
}
`;

const parsedData = JSON.parse(jsonData);

console.log(parsedData);
console.log("Name:", parsedData.name);
console.log("Level:", parsedData.level);

Try Breaking the JSON
Experiment with these:
Remove quotes from a key.
Add a trailing comma.
Change true to True.
Add a comment.
Remove a comma between properties.
Run the code after each change and observe the error.

E. Converting Between JSON and JavaScript Objects
JSON.stringify() converts a JavaScript object into a JSON string.
JSON.parse() converts a JSON string back into a JavaScript object.
%%js

// CODE_RUNNER: Convert a JavaScript object to JSON and back.

const user = {
    username: "PLAYER_1",
    password: 12345,
    isOnline: false
};

const jsonString = JSON.stringify(user);

console.log("JSON string:");
console.log(jsonString);

const parsedUser = JSON.parse(jsonString);

console.log("JavaScript object:");
console.log(parsedUser);

Try It
Modify the object by:
Adding a new property.
Adding an array.
Adding a nested object.

F. Complex: Nested Character Object
Game characters can contain multiple levels of information.
%%js

const character = {
    name: "Steve",
    username: "PLAYER_1",
    character_info: {
        level: 10,
        class: "Warrior",
        health: 100
    },
    skills: ["Attack", "Defend", "Heal"]
};

console.log("Name:", character.name);
console.log("Username:", character.username);
console.log("Level:", character.character_info.level);
console.log("Class:", character.character_info.class);
console.log("Health:", character.character_info.health);
console.log("Skills:", character.skills);

This demonstrates:
A main object.
A nested object.
An array.
Accessing nested properties.

